    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mendoza's settlement of 1536 was abandoned after attacks by indigenous people, and Garay's refounding of 1580 became a port that depended on trade and, under Spanish restrictions, on contraband.</li><li>The city became the capital of the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s independence with the May Revolution of 1810, and was federalised in 1880 as the seat of the national government.</li><li>From 1880 to 1930 it received millions of immigrants, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and in the 1970s it suffered political violence and the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> that began in 1976.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name came from the Virgin of Buen Ayre, the Madonna of Bonaria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, whom Spanish sailors invoked for fair winds; for many years it was wrongly attributed to a Sancho del Campo, until Eduardo Madero's research in 1882.</p></aside>
    </section>

