    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1536</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a> establishes the first settlement, in what is now the San Telmo district</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1580</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay">Juan de Garay</a> founds the permanent settlement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">late 18th century</span><span class="rf-text">Charles III declares Buenos Aires an open port and makes it capital of the new Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1810</span><span class="rf-text">The May Revolution removes the viceroy Cisneros and begins the Argentine War of Independence</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1880</span><span class="rf-text">The city is federalised and becomes the seat of government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The demonstration of 17 October at the Plaza de Mayo marks the emergence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1976</span><span class="rf-text">A coup led by General Jorge Videla begins the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1996</span><span class="rf-text">The city elects its first head of government, after the constitutional amendment of 1994 gave it autonomy</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay.jpg" alt="Juan de Garay" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay">Juan de Garay</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buenos Aires lies on the south-western shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, in the pampa region. The land was formerly crossed by streams and lagoons, among them the Maldonado, Vega, Medrano, Cildañez and White, some of which were later filled in and others enclosed in pipes. Parts of the city, among them the main port and the Puerto Madero district, stand on land reclaimed along the river's coast.</p><p>In 1516 Juan Díaz de Solís, sailing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was the first European to reach the Río de la Plata. He was killed in an attack by the Charrúa in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. The city was first established on 2 February 1536 by a Spanish expedition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a>, in what is today the San Telmo district, as the Ciudad de Nuestra Señora Santa María del Buen Ayre. The name honoured the Virgin of Buen Ayre, the Madonna of Bonaria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, whom Spanish sailors invoked for fair winds; the chaplain of Mendoza's expedition, a devotee, suggested it. Indigenous people attacked the settlement, and it was abandoned in the early 1540s.</p><p>A second and permanent settlement was established on 11 June 1580 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay">Juan de Garay</a>, who sailed down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-river">Paraná River</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>. He called it the Ciudad de la Santísima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> y Puerto de Santa María del Buen Aire, keeping Mendoza's name, and the short form Buenos Aires became common in the 17th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Charles III of Spain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_III_of_Spain_high_resolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anton Raphael Mengs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From its earliest days Buenos Aires depended on trade. For most of the 17th century Spanish ships were menaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and trade with the American colonies ran through convoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, the only port allowed to trade with them, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and from there to the other cities of the viceroyalty. Goods took a long time to reach Buenos Aires, and the taxes on transport made them prohibitive. A contraband trade that the authorities tolerated grew up inside the viceroyalties and with the Portuguese, and it left a deep resentment among the porteños, as the people of the city are called, towards the Spanish authorities.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> eased the restrictions in stages and finally declared Buenos Aires an open port in the late 18th century. He also split a region from the Viceroyalty of Perú to create the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, with Buenos Aires as its capital. In the second half of the 19th century customs duties, foreign trade, the fertile pampas and the railways increased the city's economic power, as raw materials flowed into its factories.</p><p>From 1880 to 1930 it was a leading destination for immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the number of porteños tripled between 1887 and 1915, from 500,000 to 1.5 million. In those years the main avenues were built, the Colón Theater became one of the leading opera venues, and the first underground line opened in 1913. A second construction boom from 1945 to 1980 reshaped the centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers.jpg" alt="Santiago de Liniers" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers">Santiago de Liniers</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santiago_de_Liniers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buenos Aires was under the Spanish crown from 1536. Spanish rule passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> period (1536 to 1700) to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> period (1700 to 1808) and the Bonaparte period of 1808 to 1810. In 1806 the British invaded and took the city, and an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers">Santiago de Liniers</a> defeated them; the viceroy Rafael Sobremonte had escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and named it capital. Liniers, chosen as the new viceroy, repelled a second British invasion in 1807.</p><p>In 1810 the criollo armies supported the May Revolution, which removed the viceroy Baltasar Hidalgo de Cisneros and began the Argentine War of Independence. The government was held first by two Juntas, then by two triumvirates and then by a Supreme Director, and independence was declared in 1816 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a>. Buenos Aires was never again under royalist rule. Its relations with the provinces were tense: the missions it sent out after 1810 to obtain their approval often ended in violent clashes. The city was the capital of Buenos Aires Province and for a time of the seceded State of Buenos Aires.</p><p>The question of its political status was fought out on the battlefield more than once. It was settled in 1880, when the city was federalised and became the seat of the national government, its mayor appointed by the president. The Casa Rosada became the president's seat. A constitutional amendment of 1994 granted the city autonomy, and on 30 June 1996 voters chose their first elected head of government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buenos Aires is laid out on a rectangular grid of square blocks 110 metres long, called manzanas. The city was conceived around the Plaza de Mayo, the colony's administrative centre: the Casa Rosada stands to the east, the Cathedral, on the same site since colonial times, to the north, and the Cabildo to the west. It is divided into 48 official barrios, a division originally based on Catholic parishes.</p><p>Most porteños have European origins, mostly from the Andalusian, Galician, Asturian and Basque regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and from several Italian regions. In the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> 2.1 per cent of the people of the city, 61,876, declared themselves Indigenous or first-generation descendants of Indigenous people, and 15,764 identified as Afro-Argentine. The Jewish community of Greater Buenos Aires numbers around 250,000, the largest in the country. At the beginning of the 20th century Buenos Aires was the second-largest Catholic city in the world, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>Its Spanish, called Rioplatense, is influenced by the dialects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>. The Italian immigrants, who spoke mostly Neapolitan, Sicilian and Genoese, created a mixture with Spanish called cocoliche, which declined around the 1950s. Lunfardo, a slang that began among the prison population, spread to all porteños and draws on Italian dialects, Brazilian Portuguese and African and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> was commonly heard in the Balvanera garment district and in Villa Crespo until the 1960s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> is known as the birthplace of tango.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In poor areas health conditions in the 19th century were appalling, with high rates of tuberculosis. Physicians and politicians blamed the poor themselves and their crowded tenements, the conventillos. Floods damaged the city's infrastructure in 1908, and many streams were then channelled and later enclosed. The city was blockaded by naval forces twice, by the French from 1838 to 1840 and by an Anglo-French expedition from 1845 to 1848; neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> brought the government to the negotiating table.</p><p>On 16 June 1955 a splinter faction of the Navy bombed the Plaza de Mayo area and killed 364 civilians. It was the only time the city was attacked from the air. A military uprising deposed President Perón three months later. In the 1970s the city suffered from fighting between the left-wing movements Montoneros, ERP and F.A.R. and the right-wing paramilitary group Triple A.</p><p>The coup of March 1976, led by General Jorge Videla, escalated the conflict. The "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirty-war">Dirty War</a>" resulted in 30,000 desaparecidos, people kidnapped and killed by the military during the junta's years. Their mothers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mothers-of-the-plaza-de-mayo">Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo</a>, held silent marches. The mayor appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, Osvaldo Cacciatore, planned a network of freeways that called for the razing of residential areas; three of the eight planned were built. Democracy returned in 1983. On 17 March 1992 a bomb at the Israeli Embassy killed 29 people and injured 242, and on 18 July 1994 an explosion destroyed a building housing several Jewish organisations and killed 85.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Plaza de Mayo, the scene of the May Revolution of 1810, has been a hub of political life since. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a> emerged there in the demonstration of 17 October 1945, and industrial workers of the surrounding belt of Greater Buenos Aires have been its main support since. The city has also long been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s main venue of liberal, free-trading and foreign ideas, in contrast to the more nationalistic and Catholic provinces to its north-west, and much of the country's internal tension, beginning with the centralist-federalist conflicts of the 19th century, can be traced to these views.</p><p>Its architecture is eclectic, with elements resembling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and a mixture of Colonial, Art Deco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>, Neo-Gothic and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> styles. The simple Rioplatense baroque survives in the Cathedral and the Cabildo, and the Teatro Colón was designed by Francesco Tamburini and Vittorio Meano. The Palacio Barolo, built from 1919 to 1923, was the tallest building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> at the time. On 30 September 2009 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared tango part of the world's cultural heritage.</p><p>Buenos Aires is the capital and largest city of Argentina. Its population has hovered around 3 million since 1947 while the surrounding districts grew more than fivefold, to around 10 million. Cardinal Jorge Bergoglio, its archbishop, was elected pope as Francis in 2013.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Buenos Aires, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1536 and refounded in 1580. It was a viceregal capital, began Argentine independence in 1810, received millions of immigrants and lived through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of 1976 to 1983. It is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>.</em></p>

